WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.880
Imagine you are struggling to learn a highly complex topic, like maybe you're prepping for a grueling certification exam or, you know, trying to grasp the architecture of a new coding language.

00:11.580 --> 00:15.000
Or just hitting an absolute wall on a college physics class. We've all been there.

00:15.060 --> 00:22.760
Right, exactly. So you're taking practice quiz after practice quiz, your scores are totally stagnant, and the frustration is just mounting.

00:23.140 --> 00:24.380
It's the worst feeling.

00:24.380 --> 00:37.260
It really is. But then an AI tutor enters the picture. It ingests your entire history of past mistakes, processes all that data, and delivers this deceptively simple directive.

00:38.100 --> 00:39.140
Ooh, this sounds promising.

00:39.580 --> 00:45.940
Well, it tells you that if you just go back and review three highly specific concepts, you will pass the final test.

00:46.060 --> 00:46.380
Wow.

00:46.380 --> 00:56.200
Yeah. It sounds like the ultimate educational shortcut, right? Like a perfectly optimized path to success tailored to your unique cognitive footprint.

00:56.580 --> 01:03.180
It does. But, you know, a new wave of research is asking a rather uncomfortable question about these systems.

01:03.560 --> 01:03.780
Right.

01:04.060 --> 01:11.560
What if the AI isn't actually giving you a roadmap to learning? What if it's merely exploiting a loophole in its own logic?

01:11.560 --> 01:23.400
Okay. Let's unpack this. Our focus for this deep dive is a comprehensive, fiercely analytical paper published in November 2026 by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:23.580 --> 01:25.280
Yes, and it's a fascinating read.

01:25.540 --> 01:36.000
It really is. The paper takes aim at a recently introduced educational AI framework known as RA4KT. That stands for Reverse Attribution for Knowledge Tracing.

01:36.060 --> 01:37.140
Right. RA4KT.

01:37.140 --> 01:48.280
So whether you are a student, a professional learning new skills, or just insanely curious about how AI makes decisions, this deep dive will change how you look at algorithmic recommendations.

01:48.520 --> 01:54.600
Because we are going to separate the difference between hacking an AI's prediction and actually helping someone learn.

01:54.740 --> 02:00.160
Exactly. Because, I mean, the allure of a perfectly optimized study plan is undeniable, right?

02:00.160 --> 02:08.240
Oh, absolutely. Especially in an era where we rely on algorithmic recommendations for nearly every aspect of our lives.

02:08.300 --> 02:11.260
Like navigating traffic or managing our diets.

02:11.500 --> 02:19.940
Exactly. We inherently want to believe that a complex computational process yields a practically feasible action in the real world.

02:20.080 --> 02:22.840
We want the math to equal reality.

02:22.840 --> 02:32.260
Right. And the distinction between an algorithm finding a mathematically sound pathway within its own code and a human being actually executing that pathway.

02:32.420 --> 02:35.960
Well, that is the central conflict in modern educational technology.

02:37.040 --> 02:42.400
So before we can effectively critique this algorithm, we really need a solid grasp of what it's building on.

02:42.460 --> 02:44.620
And that is standard knowledge tracing.

02:45.180 --> 02:46.820
Yes, knowledge tracing or KT.

02:46.820 --> 02:54.000
And for my reading of the sources, this isn't some flashy new concept born entirely from the recent generative AI boom, is it?

02:54.360 --> 02:59.640
Not at all. I mean, the lineage of knowledge tracing stretches way back to 1994.

02:59.960 --> 03:01.040
Wow. 1994.

03:01.580 --> 03:03.660
Yeah, with Corbett and Anderson's foundational work.

03:04.420 --> 03:06.600
Initially, it relied on Bayesian networks.

03:07.200 --> 03:10.820
Okay. And what exactly does that mean in this context?

03:10.820 --> 03:18.920
Well, they're essentially statistical models that update the probability of a student knowing a specific skill every single time they answer a question.

03:19.180 --> 03:19.620
Oh, I see.

03:20.000 --> 03:28.900
Yeah. So it was a way to map observable behaviors like getting a quiz question right or wrong to a hidden latent state of knowledge in the student's brain.

03:29.140 --> 03:37.640
So it's basically building a continuously updating profile of my competence based on the breadcrumbs I leave behind in a learning platform.

03:37.720 --> 03:39.480
That captures the essence perfectly, yes.

03:39.680 --> 03:39.960
Okay.

03:39.960 --> 03:45.600
But over the past decade, that basic statistical approach has been supercharged by deep learning.

03:45.600 --> 03:47.400
Right. Of course. Everything has.

03:47.800 --> 03:52.700
Exactly. Around 2015, we saw the introduction of deep knowledge tracing.

03:53.080 --> 04:00.180
And this utilized recurrent neural networks to process vastly longer and, you know, more complex sequences of student interactions.

04:00.520 --> 04:02.580
So it's not just looking at a single question anymore.

04:02.700 --> 04:02.940
Right.

04:02.940 --> 04:13.080
Today's models analyze the timing, the sequence, and the really subtle patterns across thousands of interactions to forecast your future performance.

04:13.080 --> 04:16.560
They're trying to predict if you'll succeed or fail on an upcoming task.

04:16.720 --> 04:18.920
And they are incredibly adept at it.

04:18.920 --> 04:21.680
I mean, that predictive power is impressive, sure.

04:21.680 --> 04:27.080
But if I put myself in the shoes of you, the listener, a struggling student,

04:27.080 --> 04:35.180
simply being told by a highly accurate neural network that I have an 85% chance of failing tomorrow's calculus exam is profoundly unhelpful.

04:35.180 --> 04:37.480
It's actually a bit defeatist, isn't it?

04:37.740 --> 04:37.900
Yeah.

04:38.180 --> 04:41.060
A diagnosis without a prescription doesn't solve my problem.

04:41.400 --> 04:42.220
I need an intervention.

04:42.700 --> 04:49.340
And that specific limitation is the driving force behind the broader machine learning field known as algorithmic recourse.

04:49.740 --> 04:51.000
Algorithmic recourse, right.

04:51.000 --> 04:51.420
Yes.

04:51.580 --> 04:58.100
So when a model delivers an adverse prediction, whether it's predicting you will fail a test or denying your mortgage application,

04:58.380 --> 05:02.600
the immediate human response is to ask what can be done to change that outcome.

05:02.680 --> 05:03.000
Naturally.

05:03.340 --> 05:04.560
You want to know how to fix it.

05:04.820 --> 05:05.000
Right.

05:05.760 --> 05:09.640
So researchers attempt to answer this through counterfactual explanations.

05:10.240 --> 05:15.640
They literally ask the model what theoretical changes to the input data would result in a favorable output.

05:15.940 --> 05:16.160
Okay.

05:16.220 --> 05:17.300
So what if scenarios?

05:17.640 --> 05:18.000
Exactly.

05:18.000 --> 05:22.780
An algorithmic recourse takes that concept and adds a crucial layer of constraint.

05:23.020 --> 05:28.580
It demands that those theoretical changes actually translate into feasible real-world actions.

05:29.100 --> 05:31.940
Which brings us directly back to the RA4KT framework.

05:32.100 --> 05:32.380
Right.

05:32.760 --> 05:40.120
So the authors of this model took that concept of algorithmic recourse and applied it to the sequence of a student's past answers.

05:40.480 --> 05:41.080
Yes, they did.

05:41.080 --> 05:49.480
So the architecture basically takes a student who is forecasted to fail a target concept and poses a counterfactual scenario to the prediction engine.

05:50.040 --> 05:52.240
It asks the model a very specific question.

05:52.720 --> 06:00.280
It asks, what is the smallest number of past incorrect answers that, if we retroactively flip them to correct answers,

06:00.280 --> 06:04.180
would cause you to change your prediction from failure to success?

06:04.580 --> 06:04.800
Yes.

06:04.840 --> 06:05.040
Okay.

06:05.340 --> 06:10.240
The mechanism is entirely focused on searching for a modified historical record.

06:10.340 --> 06:13.160
Which Flyxion calls H-prime in the critique, right?

06:13.380 --> 06:13.740
Correct.

06:14.300 --> 06:19.500
The algorithm combs through the student's past, isolates instances where the student made an error,

06:19.500 --> 06:26.260
and simulates what the predictor would output if those errors were magically erased and replaced with perfect competence.

06:26.420 --> 06:27.300
So it's wiped away.

06:27.440 --> 06:27.820
Exactly.

06:28.300 --> 06:35.900
The objective is to push the model's confidence above a specific boundary, termed the flip threshold, which they set at 0.5.

06:35.960 --> 06:37.060
Okay, so 50%.

06:37.060 --> 06:37.540
Right.

06:37.940 --> 06:43.080
The system wants to transition from a state of predicting failure to a state of predicting success.

06:43.280 --> 06:49.240
Wait, so instead of predicting the future, it's rewriting the past to see what changes the AI's mind?

06:49.840 --> 06:51.400
That is precisely what it's doing.

06:51.860 --> 06:53.800
It's essentially a time travel scenario.

06:54.100 --> 06:56.780
I mean, it reminds me of failing a final exam on a Friday,

06:57.160 --> 07:01.900
and then having a computer calculate that if I had only eaten a proper breakfast on Tuesday,

07:02.300 --> 07:05.360
and reviewed my flashcards on Thursday, I would have passed.

07:05.720 --> 07:07.260
That is a great way to look at it.

07:07.260 --> 07:11.540
And then the AI goes back, overwrites the timeline to indicate I did those things,

07:12.140 --> 07:14.960
feeds that altered timeline into its own prediction engine,

07:15.340 --> 07:18.820
and the engine celebrates the fact that I am now projected to pass.

07:18.820 --> 07:23.300
The time travel analogy perfectly highlights the temporal manipulation at play here.

07:23.700 --> 07:24.440
It's a bit wild.

07:24.620 --> 07:25.020
It is.

07:25.720 --> 07:28.980
Furthermore, the algorithm doesn't just flip answers indiscriminately.

07:29.360 --> 07:32.440
It applies a series of mathematical penalties to constrain its search.

07:32.700 --> 07:33.580
Okay, what kind of penalties?

07:34.180 --> 07:38.820
Well, for one, it penalizes the sheer volume of changes to keep the edit set sparse.

07:39.060 --> 07:41.560
Meaning it tries to find the minimum number of interventions?

07:41.880 --> 07:42.060
Right.

07:42.060 --> 07:44.300
It doesn't want to flip a hundred answers if three will do.

07:45.300 --> 07:47.960
It also utilizes a predefined curriculum graph.

07:48.400 --> 07:49.460
A curriculum graph.

07:49.580 --> 07:51.020
What is that, like a map of topics?

07:51.280 --> 07:51.680
Exactly.

07:51.880 --> 07:55.820
It's a topological map of how different educational topics relate to one another.

07:56.380 --> 08:00.900
And the math heavily favors flipping answers that correspond to concepts located in close

08:00.900 --> 08:03.720
proximity to the target concept on that graph.

08:04.120 --> 08:04.520
Makes sense.

08:04.580 --> 08:06.740
You would want to jump to a totally unrelated subject.

08:06.960 --> 08:07.120
Right.

08:07.440 --> 08:12.400
And perhaps most importantly, it adheres to a strict rule where it can only upgrade incorrect

08:12.400 --> 08:13.700
answers to correct ones.

08:14.180 --> 08:16.200
So it can't make you forget something you already knew.

08:16.280 --> 08:16.640
Exactly.

08:16.820 --> 08:20.760
It cannot simulate the student suddenly forgetting a skill they previously demonstrated.

08:20.760 --> 08:21.680
Okay.

08:21.860 --> 08:26.600
So I noticed in the paper that after running this complex penalized search through the

08:26.600 --> 08:30.920
student's history, the framework generates a very specific three-part output.

08:31.340 --> 08:31.540
Yes.

08:31.680 --> 08:32.380
The output triple.

08:32.980 --> 08:35.980
It delivers one, the sparse edit set of flipped answers.

08:36.480 --> 08:40.140
Two, the educational concepts tied to those specific answers.

08:40.560 --> 08:45.840
And three, a linear order of those concepts based on traversing that curriculum graph we just

08:45.840 --> 08:46.260
talked about.

08:46.360 --> 08:46.780
That's right.

08:46.780 --> 08:51.020
And then the system packages this numbered list and presents it to the student as an

08:51.020 --> 08:53.300
actionable, personalized study plan.

08:53.620 --> 08:56.460
And the presentation of that output is the critical juncture here.

08:56.700 --> 09:00.700
The algorithm claims to offer a prioritized pathway to mastery.

09:01.120 --> 09:01.380
Right.

09:01.520 --> 09:04.460
Explicitly designed to minimize the learning burden on the student.

09:04.640 --> 09:04.920
Yes.

09:05.560 --> 09:10.940
I can completely empathize with why a system like this would be commercially and psychologically

09:10.940 --> 09:11.460
appealing.

09:12.020 --> 09:16.520
When you are lost in a dense subject, the cognitive overload is just paralyzing.

09:16.520 --> 09:17.180
It's overwhelming.

09:17.760 --> 09:22.440
Having an authoritative data-driven system cut through all that noise and provide a localized

09:22.440 --> 09:25.260
step-by-step itinerary feels like a lifeline.

09:25.260 --> 09:27.600
It feels like exactly what you need.

09:28.220 --> 09:32.900
But if we transition to Flyxion's critique, this is where the entire edifice starts to show

09:32.900 --> 09:35.060
severe structural cracks.

09:36.000 --> 09:36.520
Okay.

09:36.680 --> 09:40.380
So we have a hypothetical path that makes the AI optimistic.

09:41.040 --> 09:42.300
So what does this all mean?

09:43.000 --> 09:44.620
Does it actually help the student learn?

09:44.840 --> 09:46.000
That is the question.

09:46.000 --> 09:46.400
Right.

09:46.960 --> 09:51.540
The algorithm is taking a hypothetical scenario that satisfies its internal math and projecting

09:51.540 --> 09:55.280
it onto the physical world as a guaranteed teaching strategy.

09:55.820 --> 10:00.340
And Flyxion argues there is a massive, unbridgeable logical gap here.

10:01.060 --> 10:06.020
Finding an altered history does not identify the expected real-world outcome of a future teaching

10:06.020 --> 10:06.500
action.

10:06.900 --> 10:07.120
Okay.

10:07.180 --> 10:08.080
So break that down for us.

10:08.140 --> 10:08.500
Why not?

10:08.500 --> 10:14.060
Well, Flyxion breaks down the gap into three fundamental realities of human learning.

10:14.560 --> 10:15.860
Let's start with the issue of timing.

10:16.360 --> 10:16.480
Okay.

10:16.780 --> 10:17.100
Timing.

10:17.500 --> 10:22.560
The algorithm's entire operation is based on editing the evidence a predictor sees regarding

10:22.560 --> 10:23.660
past events.

10:23.840 --> 10:24.380
Right?

10:24.460 --> 10:25.600
It changes the history.

10:25.600 --> 10:26.160
Yeah.

10:26.240 --> 10:27.480
The time travel we talked about.

10:28.040 --> 10:28.340
Exactly.

10:28.920 --> 10:31.860
But a teaching action is a future-facing intervention.

10:32.360 --> 10:38.700
The underlying AI model was trained exclusively to forecast subsequent responses based on a fixed,

10:38.940 --> 10:39.940
immutable past.

10:39.940 --> 10:42.220
It only knows how to look at what already happened.

10:42.540 --> 10:42.640
Right.

10:42.880 --> 10:43.040
Right.

10:43.460 --> 10:47.820
Its objective function contains absolutely no mechanisms for predicting the causal effects

10:47.820 --> 10:52.040
of inserting a newly generated instructional event into a student's life.

10:52.300 --> 10:55.020
Wait, so the AI is confusing a map for the territory.

10:55.320 --> 10:55.760
Precisely.

10:56.160 --> 11:00.280
The model is acting as an observer of the past, not a participant in the future.

11:00.540 --> 11:00.740
Okay.

11:00.880 --> 11:05.820
And the second reason Flyxion highlights involves state reproduction, which I found incredibly

11:05.820 --> 11:06.180
compelling.

11:06.320 --> 11:06.560
Yes.

11:06.680 --> 11:08.180
State reproduction is a huge flaw.

11:08.180 --> 11:13.200
Because just because the AI flips a bit in its database to indicate I correctly answered

11:13.200 --> 11:17.520
a fractions question three weeks ago, that doesn't mean that assigning me a fractions

11:17.520 --> 11:21.940
worksheet today will perfectly recreate that exact state of cognitive competence.

11:22.500 --> 11:28.100
The AI is conflating a computational representation of competence with the physical acquisition of that competence.

11:28.360 --> 11:28.520
Right.

11:28.820 --> 11:34.700
If a student genuinely answers a question correctly in the real world, it is an outward manifestation

11:34.700 --> 11:36.740
of an internal latent understanding.

11:36.740 --> 11:38.080
They actually know it.

11:38.180 --> 11:38.740
They know it.

11:39.100 --> 11:43.840
But instructing a student to study that topic today involves a massive array of unmodeled

11:43.840 --> 11:44.640
human variables.

11:45.320 --> 11:45.500
Like what?

11:46.080 --> 11:51.280
Like their current cognitive load, the quality of the instructional materials you give them,

11:51.420 --> 11:55.820
their physical fatigue, and their baseline prerequisite knowledge at this exact moment.

11:56.040 --> 11:56.180
Yeah.

11:56.880 --> 12:02.060
Changing a zero to a one in a matrix totally bypasses all of those human friction points.

12:02.140 --> 12:02.480
It does.

12:02.480 --> 12:06.420
It's like changing the numbers on your bank statement to show you have a million dollars

12:06.420 --> 12:09.880
and then asking the bank's algorithm if you qualify for a mortgage.

12:09.880 --> 12:11.740
That is a perfect analogy.

12:12.260 --> 12:14.980
The algorithm looks at the screenshot, says, yes, you're approved.

12:14.980 --> 12:18.240
But that doesn't mean you actually have the money to buy the house.

12:18.360 --> 12:18.560
Right.

12:18.880 --> 12:24.020
Altering the input to trick the evaluator doesn't magically alter the physical reality of your

12:24.020 --> 12:24.500
finances.

12:24.860 --> 12:25.160
Exactly.

12:25.640 --> 12:29.960
And that cuts directly to the core of the issue, which aligns with Flyxion's third point

12:29.960 --> 12:35.080
regarding the distinction between mathematical monotonicity and practical feasibility.

12:35.920 --> 12:36.440
Monotonicity.

12:36.440 --> 12:37.300
Let's define that.

12:37.720 --> 12:44.700
So the RA4KT authors implement a constraint they term reality, which strictly limits the

12:44.700 --> 12:47.940
algorithm to flipping incorrect answers to correct ones.

12:48.020 --> 12:49.260
The math only goes up.

12:49.380 --> 12:50.360
That's monotonicity.

12:50.500 --> 12:54.220
And they leverage this constraint to justify using the phrase actionable recourse.

12:54.860 --> 12:55.280
They do.

12:56.080 --> 13:01.120
However, simply enforcing a mathematical rule that variables can only increase does not answer

13:01.120 --> 13:04.400
the fundamental questions of real world feasibility.

13:04.400 --> 13:06.980
Right, because the real world doesn't just go up automatically.

13:07.420 --> 13:07.740
Exactly.

13:08.580 --> 13:12.100
Are the necessary study materials even accessible to this specific student?

13:12.340 --> 13:15.620
Do they have the required time before the exam to learn this?

13:15.940 --> 13:19.200
Are these concepts even within their cognitive reach right now?

13:19.380 --> 13:23.160
The algorithm's constraints are purely numerical, not pedagogical.

13:23.680 --> 13:29.380
If we connect this to the bigger picture, this naturally brings up the broader issue of causal

13:29.380 --> 13:29.880
inference.

13:30.260 --> 13:31.280
Yeah, causal inference.

13:31.280 --> 13:37.100
Because the RA4KT paper boldly uses the word causality informed right in its title.

13:37.620 --> 13:38.000
Yes, it does.

13:38.200 --> 13:42.100
And to a layperson, that suggests a level of scientific certainty about cause and effect

13:42.100 --> 13:43.000
in the real world.

13:43.760 --> 13:48.360
Yet, as you read deeper into the methodology, that certainty just seems to evaporate.

13:48.740 --> 13:52.600
The application of causal inference terminology here is highly contentious.

13:53.100 --> 13:58.620
In statistical sciences, there is an absolute firewall between observational data, which is just

13:58.620 --> 14:02.640
recognizing that two variables tend to move together, and interventional data.

14:03.000 --> 14:05.200
Which is what a study plan actually is.

14:05.440 --> 14:05.800
Exactly.

14:06.660 --> 14:11.400
Interventional data requires understanding the mechanism of how changing one variable physically

14:11.400 --> 14:12.360
impacts another.

14:12.900 --> 14:17.520
To predict the outcome of a study plan, you require a robust causal model of human cognition.

14:18.100 --> 14:19.340
And this AI doesn't have that?

14:19.340 --> 14:21.200
This AI possesses no such model.

14:21.620 --> 14:23.880
It solely maps patterns in sequential data.

14:23.880 --> 14:24.280
Wow.

14:24.700 --> 14:29.480
And, you know, the authors do eventually concede this point deep within the Eshin section of

14:29.480 --> 14:29.880
the paper.

14:30.060 --> 14:30.480
They do.

14:30.680 --> 14:30.920
Yeah.

14:31.040 --> 14:36.080
They acknowledge that their use of causal strictly refers to the sensitivity of the model's

14:36.080 --> 14:40.560
own predictions to altered inputs, not to real learning outcomes.

14:40.560 --> 14:44.900
I see this so often in academic publishing, and it is endlessly frustrating.

14:45.740 --> 14:50.800
They bury the severe limitations and caveats on page 8, but the title, the abstract, and

14:50.800 --> 14:55.420
the press releases are saturated with confident, definitive vocabulary.

14:55.880 --> 15:00.540
Words like actionable teaching guidance and reduced learning burden.

15:00.740 --> 15:00.940
Yes.

15:01.520 --> 15:06.460
It creates a vast disparity between what the mathematics actually demonstrate and what the marketing

15:06.460 --> 15:11.140
implies to educators and institutions who might, you know, actually purchase this technology.

15:11.660 --> 15:14.360
The marketing definitely outpaces the science here.

15:14.940 --> 15:21.620
But, okay, even if we set aside the philosophical debate over whether modifying a past state equates

15:21.620 --> 15:25.900
to a future intervention, we are still left with the empirical claims of the paper.

15:26.340 --> 15:26.860
Right.

15:26.960 --> 15:27.920
The results they published.

15:28.040 --> 15:28.160
Yeah.

15:28.300 --> 15:32.120
Because the authors reported exceptionally high success rates for their framework.

15:32.120 --> 15:39.160
So, if the premise is philosophically flawed, how did the RA4KT paper report such great results?

15:40.040 --> 15:44.200
To understand how they arrived at those numbers, despite the theoretical flaws, we have to examine

15:44.200 --> 15:46.880
the architecture of their evaluation process.

15:47.400 --> 15:49.020
Let's scrutinize that yardstick then.

15:49.580 --> 15:53.580
Because the evaluation design seems to be where the most significant sleight of hand occurs.

15:54.080 --> 15:56.660
I was reading how they define and measure validity.

15:57.500 --> 15:58.960
Yes, the validity metric.

15:58.960 --> 16:05.420
They deem a generated study plan as valid if the edited sequence of answers successfully forces

16:05.420 --> 16:10.760
the predictor's output probability for the target concept above a strict evaluation threshold.

16:11.020 --> 16:13.200
Which they set at 0.8 or 80%.

16:13.200 --> 16:13.600
Right.

16:14.200 --> 16:20.880
But, wait, the algorithm's entire search function was explicitly engineered and mathematically optimized

16:20.880 --> 16:24.660
to find sequences that raise that exact output probability.

16:25.120 --> 16:28.560
The circularity of that evaluation metric is astonishing.

16:28.960 --> 16:29.540
It is.

16:29.540 --> 16:34.380
By reporting a high validity rate based on that exact metric, the authors have essentially

16:34.380 --> 16:39.960
proven nothing more than the fact that their optimization algorithm functions as programmed.

16:40.200 --> 16:40.600
Exactly.

16:41.080 --> 16:47.160
It is an exercise in an optimizer successfully beating a predictor with both components sealed

16:47.160 --> 16:49.740
inside the exact same theoretical black box.

16:49.740 --> 16:54.860
It provides zero evidence of external validity or educational efficacy.

16:55.160 --> 16:58.420
It's essentially letting the algorithm grade its own homework.

16:58.740 --> 17:02.260
That is exactly what it is doing, grading its own homework.

17:02.520 --> 17:07.440
I also noticed this circular logic heavily infects their measurement of pedagogical agreement.

17:07.820 --> 17:08.240
Yes.

17:08.440 --> 17:08.660
Right.

17:08.900 --> 17:10.740
The pedagogical relevance score.

17:10.900 --> 17:11.140
Right.

17:11.680 --> 17:17.000
They attempt to prove that the AI selects concepts that make intuitive educational sense.

17:17.000 --> 17:19.800
So, remember the curriculum graph we mentioned?

17:19.960 --> 17:21.260
The map of topics, yes.

17:21.520 --> 17:21.780
Right.

17:21.780 --> 17:26.960
During the initial search phase, the algorithm is mathematically penalized if it attempts to

17:26.960 --> 17:32.240
select concepts that are located far away from the target concept on that predefined graph.

17:32.640 --> 17:34.840
So, it's forced by its own math to stay close to home.

17:35.500 --> 17:35.940
Exactly.

17:35.940 --> 17:41.820
Then, later, during the evaluation phase, they calculate a pedagogical relevance score to

17:41.820 --> 17:43.040
prove the system's effectiveness.

17:43.660 --> 17:44.840
And how do they calculate that?

17:45.260 --> 17:50.320
The methodology for calculating that score is simply measuring how close the AI's selected

17:50.320 --> 17:54.580
concepts are to the target concept on that exact same curriculum graph.

17:55.080 --> 17:56.460
You have got to be kidding me.

17:56.800 --> 17:58.440
No, that is how they evaluated it.

