18:10.020 --> 18:13.000
That is hilarious, but incredibly accurate.

18:13.180 --> 18:17.880
You cannot claim to have discovered a natural alignment when you explicitly hard-coded the

18:17.880 --> 18:20.380
boundaries that forced that exact outcome.

18:20.840 --> 18:22.740
The system is just fulfilling its own constraints.

18:23.120 --> 18:26.240
The evaluation design consistently stacks the deck.

18:26.760 --> 18:31.160
And this extends to how they benchmarked RA-4KT against competing baseline models, too.

18:31.160 --> 18:32.100
Okay, right.

18:32.560 --> 18:34.300
The unfair baseline comparison.

18:34.640 --> 18:34.840
Yes.

18:35.620 --> 18:40.100
The paper utilizes a technique they describe as a post-hoc projection baseline.

18:40.420 --> 18:42.060
Post-hoc meaning after the fact.

18:42.240 --> 18:42.600
Exactly.

18:43.260 --> 18:47.600
So they permitted the competing algorithms to execute their search for a solution without

18:47.600 --> 18:50.740
enforcing that reality constraint we talked about earlier.

18:51.000 --> 18:53.540
The rule that they can only flip wrong answers to right ones?

18:53.720 --> 18:53.960
Right.

18:53.960 --> 18:59.160
The baseline models were allowed to explore the entire possibility space, including flipping

18:59.160 --> 19:01.200
correct answers to incorrect ones.

19:01.280 --> 19:02.720
Okay, so they let them earn wild.

19:02.840 --> 19:03.080
Yes.

19:03.640 --> 19:09.700
But then, only after the search concluded did the researchers intervene, projecting the

19:09.700 --> 19:15.220
final output back into the permitted space by deleting any edits that violated the upward-only

19:15.220 --> 19:15.660
rule.

19:15.820 --> 19:19.200
I read that and it immediately jumped out as an egregious procedural foul.

19:19.200 --> 19:24.400
Well, if you let a system build a complex strategy that fundamentally relies on a specific set

19:24.400 --> 19:30.240
of moves, and then you arbitrarily strip those moves away at the very end, the entire strategy

19:30.240 --> 19:31.220
is going to collapse.

19:31.500 --> 19:31.860
Naturally.

19:32.720 --> 19:37.200
And because of that, the validity scores for those baseline models plummeted.

19:37.340 --> 19:42.520
Meanwhile, RA-4KT was granted the massive advantage of searching strictly within the permitted

19:42.520 --> 19:44.440
boundaries from the very first calculation.

19:44.900 --> 19:47.140
It completely invalidates the comparative results.

19:47.140 --> 19:53.080
It is the equivalent of making the baseline models navigate a complex obstacle course blindfolded,

19:53.080 --> 19:58.140
while RA-4KT is allowed to study the course layout and plot an optimal route before the

19:58.140 --> 19:58.980
timer even starts.

19:59.120 --> 20:00.900
It's a completely rigged race.

20:01.220 --> 20:01.500
Right.

20:02.000 --> 20:07.440
When RA-4KT inevitably crosses the finish line first, it doesn't prove it is a fundamentally

20:07.440 --> 20:08.940
superior navigation engine.

20:09.260 --> 20:14.360
It merely proves it was operating under an entirely different, highly advantageous set of rules.

20:14.360 --> 20:19.700
A scientifically rigorous comparison requires forcing all models to optimize under identical

20:19.700 --> 20:22.760
constraints, from the moment the search initiates.

20:23.440 --> 20:26.540
Without that level playing field, the reported performance gap is meaningless.

20:27.000 --> 20:27.440
Exactly.

20:27.980 --> 20:30.400
But here's where it gets really interesting.

20:30.820 --> 20:35.300
Because the most alarming aspects of Flyxion's critique aren't just the philosophical disagreements

20:35.300 --> 20:37.660
or the flawed experimental designs.

20:37.660 --> 20:40.200
No, Flyxion didn't just find conceptual flaws.

