29:08.340 --> 29:12.380
This pattern of claiming strict adherence to rules, while the underlying math operates

29:12.380 --> 29:16.380
entirely differently, appears again in how they handle the temporal constraint.

29:16.640 --> 29:17.240
Ah, yes.

29:17.720 --> 29:20.380
The temporal constraint and the relaxation math.

29:20.380 --> 29:24.520
The paper claims a temporal constraint ensures only past steps are modified.

29:24.520 --> 29:31.260
So if a student is attempting to master a target concept at time step t, the algorithm is

29:31.260 --> 29:34.700
theoretically restricted to altering answers strictly before t.

29:35.020 --> 29:36.560
