30:00.020 --> 30:00.880
exact position.

30:01.280 --> 30:01.880
Wait, really?

30:01.880 --> 30:02.560
Yes.

30:02.560 --> 30:07.980
The published equations grant the continuous optimization process full permission to alter

30:07.980 --> 30:10.600
the outcome of the very test it is attempting to predict.

30:10.900 --> 30:13.780
Which completely violates the foundational premise of the framework.

30:14.100 --> 30:14.580
It does.

30:15.140 --> 30:20.620
And speaking of continuous optimization, I want to delve into the mechanism they use to ensure

30:20.620 --> 30:26.180
the study plan is sparse, meaning it requires the student to review the minimum possible number

30:26.180 --> 30:26.800
of concepts.

30:26.800 --> 30:32.100
This gets into the continuous relaxation of hamming distance to square distance.

30:32.600 --> 30:32.680
Right.

30:32.740 --> 30:38.540
Because they claim they want to minimize the hamming distance, the literal count of how many

30:38.540 --> 30:41.440
answers were flipped from incorrect to correct.

30:41.820 --> 30:46.580
But minimizing discrete counts is computationally expensive and difficult for neural networks.

30:46.580 --> 30:50.900
Because they rely on smooth, differentiable surfaces to calculate gradients.

30:51.220 --> 30:51.340
Right.

30:51.620 --> 30:52.160
Exactly.

30:52.700 --> 30:55.740
To bypass this, the authors employ a continuous relaxation.

30:56.300 --> 31:00.660
They replace the discrete hamming distance with a continuous squared distance.

31:00.940 --> 31:05.340
They translate the binary world of right and wrong into a landscape of fractions and decimals.

31:05.580 --> 31:06.180
Wait, hold on.

31:07.060 --> 31:10.260
I understand why continuous optimization requires smooth math.

31:10.580 --> 31:15.180
But I was looking at the implications of using squared distance, and it seems mathematically

31:15.180 --> 31:17.700
counterproductive if your goal is sparsity.

31:18.260 --> 31:19.600
Let's do the math on it.

31:19.820 --> 31:26.400
Okay, so if I want to fully flip one answer from a 0 to a 1, the distance is 1, and 1 squared

31:26.400 --> 31:26.940
is 1.

31:27.340 --> 31:27.780
Correct.

31:28.060 --> 31:34.400
But if the optimizer decides to partially flip an answer, moving it by just 0.6, the squared

31:34.400 --> 31:36.480
cost drops to 0.36.

31:37.040 --> 31:42.940
And the trap lies in how squared distance penalizes large individual movements while heavily discounting

31:42.940 --> 31:44.440
small distributed movements.

31:44.880 --> 31:45.740
Consider the alternative.

31:46.460 --> 31:51.640
What if the optimizer decides to move 10 different historical answers by a microscopic fraction

31:51.640 --> 31:53.040
of 0.1 each?

31:53.860 --> 31:55.060
The math on that is wild.

31:55.420 --> 31:57.140
0.1 squared is 0.01.

31:57.960 --> 32:03.560
Multiply that by 10 coordinates, and the total mathematical cost to the optimizer is only 0.10.

32:03.560 --> 32:10.160
The system mathematically perceives a cost of 0.10 as vastly superior to a cost of 0.36.

32:10.400 --> 32:10.500
Wow.

32:11.120 --> 32:17.320
Therefore, the optimization engine is actively incentivized to smear tiny, practically meaningless

32:17.320 --> 32:22.660
fractional changes across dozens of different past assignments, rather than executing a single

32:22.660 --> 32:25.160
decisive flip on a major misconception.

32:25.680 --> 32:31.080
So the mathematical relaxation they chose actively destroys the sparsity they claim is the core

32:31.080 --> 32:31.840
feature of the model.

32:31.840 --> 32:32.440
Exactly.

32:32.960 --> 32:37.660
They built a system designed to find a few key interventions, but they used a mathematical

32:37.660 --> 32:41.700
engine that inherently prefers making a thousand microscopic adjustments.

32:42.040 --> 32:48.260
So the internal logic actively is actively at war with the stated pedagogical goals.

32:48.600 --> 32:49.340
It really is.

32:49.720 --> 32:53.360
And this brings us to the most profound aspect of Flyxion's critique.

32:53.800 --> 32:59.020
Even if we wave a magic wand, even if the standard deviations were accurate, the curriculum graph

